        "theme":  "Agents",
        "summary":  "Security researchers at Pillar Security discovered critical vulnerabilities in Google\u0027s Agent Development Kit that allowed prompt injection attacks via pull requests and public issues, enabling attackers to trigger more privileged automation and potentially expose credentials or manipulate code reviews. Google addressed the issues after disclosure, but the flaws exposed a broader architectural risk: natural language has joined the authorization path in agentic systems. The findings highlight the need for organizations to map authority flows across interconnected agent-to-agent interactions.",
        "execSummary":  "The Google ADK vulnerabilities are a landmark case study demonstrating that AI agents create a new attack surface where natural language inputs ΓÇö not just code or credentials ΓÇö can escalate privileges and trigger sensitive automation. For enterprise security and AI platform leaders, this is a direct mandate to treat every external input to an agent (issues, tickets, emails, customer messages) as a potential injection vector, not just a data source. In customer-support contexts specifically, any agent that reads customer-submitted text and then acts on internal systems needs prompt-level sandboxing and explicit authority boundaries. The key implication: agent security architecture must be designed before deployment, not retrofitted after an incident.",
        "vendorLean":  "Google ΓÇö vulnerability disclosure is factual; Google\u0027s responsiveness noted",
        "imageUrl":  "https://www.infoworld.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/08/4204919-0-30053600-1785843869-shutterstock_2401223205.jpg"
