        "theme":  "Philosophy",
        "summary":  "Columnist Matt Asay argues that AI should only be applied when it serves a meaningful, outcome-linked purpose ΓÇö distinguishing between automating genuinely valuable processes versus proliferating low-value or non-existent outputs. He proposes a litmus test: if the output would not be missed if it stopped existing, it is not an automation opportunity but a cancellation opportunity. The piece pushes back against using AI to generate content and workflows simply because the technology is available.",
        "execSummary":  "This commentary provides a sharp and practical decision framework for AI investment prioritization that applies directly to enterprise and customer-support contexts: before automating anything with AI, leaders should be able to name a concrete, loss-worthy outcome that justifies the deployment. The piece challenges the prevailing trend of AI-for-AI\u0027s-sake and implicitly calls out budget waste in initiatives that automate low-value or performative outputs. For senior leaders, the actionable insight is to require a \u0027what would we lose if this stopped?\u0027 test for every AI project in the portfolio review cycle. The caveat is that the framework is intentionally blunt and does not account for strategic, longer-horizon AI capability building that may not yield immediate measurable outcomes.",
